window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"16-04","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"16-04-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-04-01/","title_he":"א – דרך אכילת הפירות שלא להפסידם","title_en":"1. Eating the Fruit Without Wasting It","body_en":"<p>Regarding the fruit of Shevi'it it is said (Vayikra 25:6-7): \"And the Sabbath produce of the land shall be food for you: for you, and for your servant and for your maid, and for your hired servant and for the settler by your side who sojourn with you. And for your cattle, and for the beasts that are in your land, shall all its increase be for food.\" And likewise it is said (Shemot 23:11): \"But the seventh year you shall let it rest and lie fallow, that the poor of your people may eat; and what they leave the beast of the field shall eat. In like manner you shall deal with your vineyard and with your olive grove.\" We have learned that the fruit of Shevi'it must be made ownerless for all, and there is no obligation to eat the fruit of Shevi'it, for it can be left in the fields for the animals and the birds. But if a person wants to use it, he must be careful to use it in the use proper to it, as it is said, \"And the Sabbath produce of the land shall be food for you\"; the Sages expounded (Pesaḥim 52b): 'for food' and not for waste. And this is the meaning of the expression 'holiness' said regarding the fruit of Shevi'it: they are sanctified and designated for eating in the manner proper to them, and not for waste. Therefore it is forbidden to feed fruit fit for human food to an animal, since this is a waste of the fruit, for human food is more precious and important than animal food (Rambam 5:5).</p>\n<p>In order not to waste the fruit of Shevi'it, one must eat it in the accepted way. Therefore one should not cook something that people are accustomed to eat raw, such as watermelon, banana and avocado. And one should not eat raw something that people are accustomed to eat cooked, such as rice and lentils (Rambam 5:3). A fruit that people are accustomed to eat mashed, such as potatoes and bananas, may be mashed. But if only a negligible minority are accustomed to this, it is forbidden to mash it, for mashing it is considered a waste compared to what is accepted. And if he erred and mashed it, he should eat it mashed, so as not to waste it entirely.</p>\n<p>A fruit that people are accustomed to squeeze in order to drink its juice may be squeezed. And one should try to squeeze all of the fruit as far as possible; but by law it may be squeezed in the way people are accustomed in all the years, even though a little of the fruit remains there that will not be eaten (the next halakha will explain how to treat what remains). But a fruit that people are not accustomed to squeeze for its juice may not be squeezed. And even when a negligible minority are accustomed to squeeze it, the prohibition remains in place, because by squeezing it one wastes it from its usual use. And if he erred and squeezed it, he should drink the juice, so as not to waste it entirely (Mishpat Kohen 85).</p>\n<p>In the past, only a fruit that many people were accustomed to squeeze in order to drink its juice was permitted to be squeezed in Shevi'it; but a fruit that only a minority were accustomed to squeeze was forbidden to be squeezed, for this was considered a waste compared to its usual way of eating. But in recent generations the ways of eating fruit have become immeasurably varied, to the point that one can say that the main custom today is that almost everything is accepted. Therefore only when a negligible minority practise a certain thing, so that it is considered a very strange way, should it be disregarded, and it is forbidden to eat fruit of Shevi'it that way. But anything that is not a negligible minority is considered the way people eat. Therefore it is enough that a small minority are accustomed to squeeze a certain fruit, or to mash it, or to eat it raw or cooked, for it to be permitted to eat it that way, since this is not a very strange custom in the eyes of most people.<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"In Mishna Shevi'it 8:2: \"To eat a thing whose way is to be eaten.\" Yerushalmi there: \"If he wished to eat raw beets or to chew raw wheat, we do not listen to him.\" Because he changes from their way of eating, and this is waste (Rash and the Gra). And so wrote the Rambam (5:3): \"And he should not change fruit from their natural state, just as he does not change with teruma and ma'aser: a thing whose way is to be eaten raw he should not eat cooked, and a thing whose way is to be eaten cooked one does not eat raw.\" And likewise they said that one does not mash or squeeze something that people are not accustomed to (Rambam, Terumot 11:2). The fundamental question is what is considered a change; and this law also bears on the laws of Shabbat, where work done in an unusual manner is forbidden rabbinically (Shabbat 92a), and on mixed vegetables in a vineyard, where what people are not accustomed to grow does not forbid (Shabbat 144b). Tosafot explained (Shabbat 92b, s.v. 'Ve-i timtzi lomar') that a custom peculiar to a certain place, if others too would act so under the same conditions, is not considered strange in other places; but if others would not act so under the same conditions, it is considered strange. And the Ritva there added that in the place where they practise it, it is not considered strange. The Ritva further wrote that something considered distinctly strange has the law of a change even in a place where some practise it. And the Ran wrote that the custom of a small place is nullified with respect to other places and is considered strange. And it seems that there is no dispute among the Rishonim, but they defined different situations of change, and the rule is that the attitude of the people of the region to the custom determines whether it is strange.<br>In the laws of Shevi'it we find that the law changes from shemita to shemita, as my teacher and master Rav Eliyahu, of blessed memory, would say: in the past he ruled not to squeeze carrots because this was strange, but afterwards he permitted it because people began to squeeze them. And so wrote the Shevitat Ha-Sadeh 11, 3, that in the past beetroot was eaten only cooked, but now people are accustomed to cut it thin and eat it raw in a salad. However, regarding the foods of vegetarians and vegans they wrote that they are strange (Mishpetei Eretz 22:4; Ketif Shevi'it 62:3); and perhaps this was true some years ago, but for the halakha these foods are not considered strange today. And in general, in the past they ruled that only a way of eating accepted by many was considered a way of eating, but it was forbidden to eat in the way of eating of the minority, for this was considered a change (Mishpat Kohen 85). And in the last generation they already ruled that the custom of a large minority is considered a custom that is not strange (Mishpetei Eretz 22:4). And it seems that today even the custom of a small minority is not strange, since eating customs have become immeasurably varied, and almost every fruit is both squeezed and mashed. Thus the way of eating today is to eat in a very varied manner, and only what is considered very strange in people's eyes, and only a negligible minority eat it that way, is considered not the way of eating. Above, 3:1, note 1, it was explained that some say there is a mitzva to eat fruit of Shevi'it. And according to most of the poskim there is no mitzva, but if one took fruit of Shevi'it it is forbidden to waste it."}]},{"slug":"16-04-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-04-02/","title_he":"ב – דין השאריות","title_en":"2. The Law of Leftovers","body_en":"<p>Fruit and parts of fruit that were left over and that nobody wants to eat any longer, as long as they are still fit for human or animal food, retain the holiness of Shevi'it, and it is forbidden to destroy them by hand. It is also forbidden to put them in a dirty bin, because putting them there destroys them from being fit for food, since there will no longer be anyone who wants to eat them (Mishpat Kohen 85). Therefore one should wrap these fruit leftovers in a plastic or paper bag and put them in the bin, for in this way they are protected there from soiling and are not placed in a degrading way. And so it is right to act even when there is nothing dirty in the private bin, in order to protect the fruit leftovers from soiling in the central bin.</p>\n<p>Some are meticulous and prepare a special vessel for leftovers of fruit of Shevi'it, and each time they place fruit leftovers there they wrap them in plastic or paper, so that the earlier leftovers that have already begun to rot will not speed up the rotting of the new leftovers. But one is not obliged to be meticulous in this, because by law it is permitted to wrap the fruit leftovers in a bag or paper and put them in the ordinary bin, for wrapping them protects them from being soiled and degraded. And it is also permitted to put them in the central bin before they rot, for it is reasonable that the bag wrapping them will continue to protect them even in the central bin and in the rubbish lorry. And if by chance the bag tears because of the compacting of the rubbish, that is something that was not intended.</p>\n<p>And so one should act with peels that are fit to eat, such as the peels of apples, cucumbers and carrots. And likewise with the outer leaves of cabbage that people are accustomed to remove. But fruit leftovers that are not fit for human or animal food, such as peels and pits of fruit that people are not accustomed to eat today, may be thrown into the rubbish bin without restriction.</p>\n<p>One who eats foods of Shevi'it should try to finish all the food he took; and he may leave leftovers of food on the plate in the way that people who are accustomed to be careful not to destroy food needlessly are accustomed to leave in all the years. These leftovers have no holiness and may be thrown into the bin. And all the more so it is permitted to wash plates and pots from leftovers of fruit of Shevi'it stuck to them. But if foods of some significance were left over, such as the amount of a whole teaspoon in one place on the plate, or the amount of one spoon in one place in the pot, they should be treated as leftovers of fruit of Shevi'it.</p>\n<p>A food that absorbed taste from fruit of Shevi'it has the law of fruit of Shevi'it: as long as it is fit for food and is in an amount that has significance, it should be put in a bag and placed in the bin that way.</p>\n<p>And the same law applies to soup cooked with parsley of Shevi'it: if an amount of significance was left over in the pot, or more soup on the plate than people who are accustomed not to destroy needlessly leave, it should be treated with the holiness of Shevi'it. However, since these are liquids, it is hard to dispose of them respectfully. Some have suggested pouring the soup leftovers into a bag, tying it and placing it so in the bin. But this suggestion may cause a mess if the bag tears or opens. Therefore the more convenient advice is to leave the soup leftovers outside the refrigerator for a day or a night, until it reaches a state in which people are accustomed not to eat it, lest it has spoiled a little. And since in any case the person does not intend to eat it, it is then permitted to pour it out as usual.<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"The Sages expounded (Pesaḥim 52b): 'for food' and not for waste, and this prohibition is from the Torah, as emerges from the Rishonim and Aḥaronim (Rabbeinu Ḥananel, Rash, Rambam, Mishpat Kohen 83). Maran Rav Kook wrote (Mishpat Kohen 85) that there is room to say that throwing away fruit of Shevi'it is considered wasting it, since it will become dirty and nobody will want to eat of it any more. And its basis is in the Gemara, Ḥullin 71a. According to this it is permitted to put the food in a bag inside the bin, for in this way it does not become dirty.<br>Some required dedicating a special vessel for leftovers of fruit of Shevi'it, because some hold that it is forbidden to degrade fruit of Shevi'it, and therefore the Sages forbade anointing with oil of Shevi'it in a bathhouse (Penei Moshe on the Yerushalmi 8, end of halakha 2). But apart from the fact that this is not agreed (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 24:14), placing the leftovers in a bag expresses respect, even though it is afterwards placed in the bin, as indeed this is the practice regarding teruma. And some were concerned lest putting the fruit into a bag speed up its rotting; but in truth this does not speed up its rotting, as Rav Dr. Levinger tested and found (Ketif Shevi'it 63, 14). Those who wish to be meticulous to put the fruit leftovers in a special vessel must not put new leftovers with old ones, for the old ones that have already begun to smell will speed up the rotting of the new ones (Ma'amar Mordechai 13:30). And the solution is to separate them with newspaper or to put the new leftovers in a bag. However, by the basic law one who wishes to be lenient and put new leftovers on old ones may, since according to the Mahari\"t 1:83 the prohibition is to destroy the fruit by hand, but indirectly it is permitted to cause loss to fruit of Shevi'it, and here it is indirect, for the rotting will begin after a while. And although some are stringent and hold that indirect loss of fruit of Shevi'it is forbidden (Mikdash David 59:5), since the law of shemita in our time is rabbinic, and some also say that the prohibition of wasting shemita fruit is rabbinic, the halakha follows the lenient view.<br>There is no holiness in the food leftovers stuck to the plate and the pot, since people are accustomed to throw them away; as we have learned in the Mishna (Terumot 11:6-8), that even leftovers of teruma about which people are not particular have no holiness. And consequently the same law applies to leftovers of fruit of Shevi'it that no longer have significance (Ketif Shevi'it 63:12). However, it is not clear where the line is between leftovers that have no significance and leftovers that have significance, and one should act as is accepted among people who are accustomed not to destroy food needlessly. And when there is a doubt, although the law of Shevi'it is rabbinic, it is right to be stringent, since there is no difficulty in this.<br>However, regarding soup leftovers there is great difficulty. Some have suggested placing them tied inside a strong plastic bag, and for many this solution is not convenient. And the advice is to leave the soup for a day or a night, until it reaches a state in which people are no longer accustomed to eat it, lest it has spoiled (see Rash Sirillo, Shevi'it 8:2; Ma'amar Mordechai 13:32). After that one may pour it out as one always does. And although some are more stringent, since Shevi'it is rabbinic one may rely on the lenient (and see Rav Yosef Tzvi Rimon, 'Shemita', pp. 202-212)."}]},{"slug":"16-04-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-04-03/","title_he":"ג – מדיני אכילת הפירות","title_en":"3. Laws of Eating the Fruit","body_en":"<p>As we have learned (in halakha 1), the Torah commanded that the fruit of Shevi'it be 'for food' and not for waste, and the meaning is that one must eat the fruit of Shevi'it in the way it is eaten; therefore it is forbidden to mash a fruit that people are not accustomed to mash, and likewise it is forbidden to give human food to an animal. And in this the mitzva of shemita differs from the other years: in all the years, although we were warned not to destroy food, it is permitted to use it for other needs; but the fruit of the shemita is meant for eating in the way it is eaten. Nevertheless, when one uses fruit of Shevi'it in the way it is eaten, the law of care against wasting it is like the law of 'bal tashḥit' (not destroying) in all the years: whatever is not considered waste in eating in the other years is permitted by law also in the shemita. However, since the fruit of Shevi'it has holiness, where possible it is desirable to be more meticulous with fruit of Shevi'it not to cause its loss.</p>\n<p>Therefore one who wants to eat half an apple may cut an apple and eat half of it, even though as a result there may be nobody who wants to eat the other half. For this is the way the apple is eaten: sometimes people eat half of it, and sometimes as a result the other half goes to waste. But it is right to be careful to keep the remaining half in a way that will increase the chance that afterwards people will want to eat it (Ma'amar Mordechai 13:33).</p>\n<p>It is permitted to give fruit of Shevi'it to a baby, even though it usually spoils part of the fruit, for this is the way it eats. But from the outset it is good to give it a little at a time, in order to reduce the part that will go to waste.<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>It is permitted to use oil of Shevi'it for frying, even though part of the oil will remain in the pan and afterwards be poured into the sink. And likewise it is permitted to put wine of Shevi'it in food, in baking and in a cooked dish, as people are accustomed in all the years (see Ketif Shevi'it 62:10-13; Ma'amar Mordechai 13:41).</p>\n<p>It is permitted to put a slice of lemon in tea to add taste to it, for this is the way it is eaten, even though afterwards it will no longer be eaten. However, since there is still fruit in it that is fit to eat, if it is possible to eat it, well and good; and if not, it must be treated as leftovers of Shevi'it. And likewise it is permitted to use tea plants and mint to flavour a drink and a dish. And at the end of the cooking, if their taste is gone, it is permitted to throw them away; and if their taste is not gone, they must be treated as leftovers of fruit of Shevi'it (Rambam 5:22; Ketif Shevi'it 62:8-9).</p>\n<p>It is permitted to peel apples, cucumbers and carrots, even though after the peeling the peels will go to waste; for we have already learned that any eating done in the way people are accustomed to eat in the other years is permitted in Shevi'it. And the peels should be placed in a bag in the bin. And those who wish to be meticulous should try not to peel fruit of Shevi'it whose peel is fit to eat.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"So wrote the Ridbaz and the Ma'amar Mordechai 13:15, relying on the Mahari\"t 1:83, that causing loss indirectly is permitted. But it seems that even according to the Mikdash David 59:5, who forbids indirect causation, there is room to permit, since this is the way it eats. It seems that those who are accustomed to use a potato-peeling machine may use it also with fruit of Shevi'it, even though in peeling by hand about 14% of the fruit is lost and with the machine about 38%, since so people are accustomed in all the years. Nevertheless, he should not put the leftovers in a dirty bin, or he should put them there inside a bag. And if possible it is good to be meticulous and use the leftovers too for food."}]},{"slug":"16-04-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-04-04/","title_he":"ד – שימושים שאינם לאכילה","title_en":"4. Uses That Are Not for Eating","body_en":"<p>The Sages said that it is permitted to use the fruit of Shevi'it for all a person's needs, as it is said (Vayikra 25:6): \"And the Sabbath produce of the land shall be food for you\" — 'for you', for all your needs. But it says 'for you for food' to teach us that the permitted benefit is a benefit similar to eating, where the benefit comes at the time the fruit is consumed. Therefore it is permitted to light a lamp with oil made from fruit of Shevi'it, because at the time the oil is consumed one benefits from the light. And likewise, when people were accustomed to dye with fruit, it was permitted to dye with it, because at the time the fruit is consumed the colour takes hold in the garment. But it is forbidden to use straw of Shevi'it that is fit for animal food for firing the oven to heat the house, or for baking or cooking, for only after they are burned does one begin to benefit from their heat (Mishna Shevi'it 8:2; Sukka 40a; Rambam 5:19; above 3:6).</p>\n<p>A person may anoint his skin with oil made from fruit of Shevi'it, for anointing is considered like drinking (Yoma 76b). In addition, at the time of the benefit the oil is consumed. But it is forbidden to anoint leather garments and leather shoes with oil of Shevi'it in order to strengthen them, for the benefit comes after the oil has been consumed. One should not anoint the body with wine or vinegar, because people are not accustomed to anoint with them (Mishna 8:2; Rambam 5:6).</p>\n<p>It is forbidden to give an animal fruit fit for human food, so as not to waste it from its proper and choicer use. And even if the animal comes by itself to eat of the fruit, he must keep it away. And even when in practice there is no person there who will eat of the fruit, even though the fruit will go to waste, it is forbidden to give it to an animal, since it is fit for people. But while the fruit is attached to the tree, even though it is fit for human food, since it is ownerless for all, if the animal went by itself to eat of it, one need not prevent it (Rambam 5:5; see above 3:8).</p>\n<p>It is forbidden to use fruit fit for human food for medicine, because medicine is meant for the sick only, and there is a waste of fruit of Shevi'it when it is used for a purpose not equal for everyone. But it is permitted to prepare a medicine for a person from fruit meant for animal food, since this is a rise from the status of animal use to human use, even though it is for the benefit of the sick only (Mishna 8:1; Sukka 40a; Rambam 5:11).<sup>4</sup></p>\n<p>Fruit of Shevi'it about which it is known from the start that there are no people who will eat it, for example because it is too small and there is no possibility of distributing it within an 'otzar beit din', and if it is not processed in a way different from the accepted one it will have to be thrown away, may be processed; for this is not a waste of it but a rescue. And if there is no possibility of making it fit for human food, it is permitted to process it so that it will serve for anointing, medicine or animal food (see Ketif Shevi'it 62:4, 5-6).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"Plants whose whole purpose is medicine have no holiness of Shevi'it. But if their purpose is animal food, it is permitted to make medicine from them, as explained above. One should not make cosmetic products for anointing from oil fit for food, for by this one causes the oil not to be fit for food, and perhaps because of this it will go to waste and will not be used for anointing either. In addition, sometimes this also reduces its quantity (Rambam 5:6). If they transgressed and made cosmetic products from oils fit to eat, it is permitted to anoint with them (Shabbat Ha-Aretz 5:6, 2).<br>A person may anoint his body with olive oil, for there is no longer a concern that it will go to waste, since now he benefits from it. And even when the purpose of the anointing is medical it is permitted, since it is an oil that people are accustomed to anoint with for pleasure. It is permitted to make cosmetic products from oils produced from fruit or seeds that are not fit for food and whose main purpose is cosmetics, and likewise it is permitted to make medical ointments from them, and they have no holiness (see Ketif Shevi'it 64:15)."}]},{"slug":"16-04-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-04-05/","title_he":"ה – מצוות בפירות שביעית וערלה ונטע רבעי","title_en":"5. Mitzvot with Fruit of Shevi'it; Orla and Neta Revai","body_en":"<p>It is permitted to use wine of Shevi'it for all the mitzvot that require a cup of blessing, such as Kiddush, a brit mila, a pidyon ha-ben and a wedding. But at Havdala it is right not to fill the cup beyond its measure as a sign of blessing, because this is a waste of wine of Shevi'it. And likewise on the Seder night, at the second of the four cups, it is right not to drip wine from the cup when saying the ten plagues and 'Detzakh Adash Be-Aḥav', because of the waste of shemita wine (Maran Rav Kook, Shabbat Ha-Aretz, Kuntres Aḥaron 22). And one should not refrain because of this from using wine of Shevi'it, for there is greater merit in drinking wine of Shevi'it than in keeping these customs, which have no strong source.<sup>5</sup></p>\n<p>It is permitted to take an etrog of Shevi'it for the mitzva of taking the lulav on Sukkot; and although the taking may cause slight damage to its peel, this is not a significant waste that one need be concerned about. And preventing the mitzva is a greater loss (Aderet, Ketif Shevi'it 63:21).</p>\n<p>Some say that one should not use oil of Shevi'it for Ḥanukka lights, since it is forbidden to benefit from Ḥanukka lights. But the main view is that it is permitted, since lighting Ḥanukka lights too is an accepted use.<sup>6</sup></p>\n<p>It is forbidden to remove orla fruit from the tree, because by this one strengthens the growth of the tree. And so that people will not stumble in eating orla fruit, one must hang a sign there warning about this. When there is a concern that the sign will not help, it is permitted to remove the flowers or the small fruit so that people will not stumble in them (Ma'amar Mordechai 6:9-10).</p>\n<p>When the tree reaches its fourth year in the shemita, the laws of neta revai apply to its fruit, but it is ownerless for all. That is, at the time the Temple stood, the fruit had to be eaten in purity in Jerusalem, or redeemed for money with which food was bought in Jerusalem and eaten in purity. And now the fruit of neta revai is redeemed on a peruta. And so that the pickers will know that they must redeem the fruit, the owner of the tree must hang a sign there warning about this (Mishna Ma'aser Sheni 5:1; Rambam, Ma'aser Sheni and Neta Revai 9:6-7; Ma'amar Mordechai 10:14).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"And some were lenient to fill the Havdala cup over its brim, to extinguish the candle with what spills, and to put some of the wine on the eyes, for so people are accustomed in all the years; and one who wishes to rely on them may. But he should not drip wine at the ten plagues, for there the dripping is intended as a destruction, corresponding to the plagues (Ma'amar Mordechai 13:44-45)."},{"n":6,"en":"See Harḥavot to Shabbat Ha-Aretz 5:8, 2, and Ketif Shevi'it 64:3. And the main view is like Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach in Minḥat Shlomo 1:42, who was lenient. And so I heard from my teacher and master Rav Eliyahu, of blessed memory, and not as they wrote in his name in Ketif Shevi'it.<br>Maran Rav Kook wrote (Shabbat Ha-Aretz, Kuntres Aḥaron 25) that although the Sages said that one who washes his hands before eating dry fruit is considered arrogant, since the Sages did not institute washing for this (Ḥullin 106a), nevertheless before eating fruit of Shevi'it it is permitted to wash the hands, because of the name of holiness called upon it (and so wrote the Ḥesed La-Alafim in Sha'arei Tzedek 17:17). And some of the meticulous act so. But most of the poskim did not mention this practice."}]},{"slug":"16-04-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-04-06/","title_he":"ו – איסור סחורה והמכירות המותרות","title_en":"6. The Prohibition of Trade and the Permitted Sales","body_en":"<p>The Torah commanded the owners of the fields to make the fruit of Shevi'it ownerless, so that anyone who wants to eat it may come and take; therefore it is self-evident that it is forbidden to trade in it. And this is what is said (Vayikra 25:6): \"And the Sabbath produce of the land shall be food for you\"; the Sages learned: \"'for food' and not for trade\" (Avoda Zara 62a). Thus one who trades in fruit of Shevi'it cancels a positive Torah mitzva.</p>\n<p>However, on the other hand we find that it is permitted to sell a little fruit of Shevi'it, and the Torah even set laws for this, as will be explained in the next halakha. It follows that the prohibition of trade is in the manner of the owners of fields and merchants, whose aim is to earn money; but it is permitted to sell a little and cheaply, in a way that the payment is mainly for the service of bringing the fruit from the field.</p>\n<p>There are three levels in this law: (a) one who trades in large quantities in order to grow rich transgresses a Torah prohibition; (b) one who sells a little to his fellow in the manner of trade transgresses a rabbinic prohibition; (c) it is permitted to sell a little and cheaply, not in the manner of trade, in a way that it will be evident that the fruit is of Shevi'it and the payment for it becomes sanctified for buying food.</p>\n<p>This is the way of the permitted sale: he should be careful not to pick at one time more than the amount a person is accustomed to bring for his household use (above 3:3). And what he picked he should not sell himself, but give to his son or his fellow, and they too should sell it occasionally and not in a fixed place in the market. And likewise a person who gathered fruit for his household use, and some fruit was left over that he does not intend to eat, may sell it occasionally. And some say that in all forms of sale it is forbidden to sell more than the amount of three meals.</p>\n<p>The Sages stated another condition: one who sells fruit of Shevi'it should not sell it by measure of volume or weight or by number of fruit, in the manner of trade, but should sell it by estimate. In this way it will be sold cheaply, and people will remember that it is fruit of Shevi'it and be careful about its holiness, and the main payment is for the service of bringing it (Mishna 8:3, Yerushalmi there; Rambam 6:3).</p>\n<p>And one should not ask why the Sages did not forbid a small sale lest it lead to trade that is forbidden by the Torah. For the small sale helped the eating of the fruit of Shevi'it: sometimes a person who gathered ten kilos of apples for his household use preferred to sell some of them in order to buy other foods with the proceeds instead of eating apples all day. And likewise this benefited people who, because of their busyness or weakness, found it hard to reach the fields, and by this they could receive fruit of Shevi'it at a reduced price. And presumably poor people engaged in this, for it did not pay the rich to pick a small quantity and receive a small payment for it, but for the poor it was a blessing. However, so that people would not come to forbidden trade, the Sages established that even one who sells a little must depart from the usual way of trade.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"Mishna Shevi'it 7:3: \"For one does not trade in fruit of Shevi'it… And he may not take field vegetables and sell them in the market, but he gathers and his son sells for him. If he took for himself and had some left over, he may sell them.\" I wrote according to the commentary of the Rash there; Rambam 6:1-2; Tosafot Avoda Zara 62a, s.v. 'Nimtza'; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 25:4-9. And even when one sells in a permitted way, it is forbidden to sell regularly in the market (Yerushalmi there, brought in Tosafot Sukka 39a, s.v. 'Ve-leitiv'; Rivmatz, Shevi'it 7:3, Ritva, Shabbat Ha-Aretz 6:1, 4, 4). Thus the sale that was permitted is a sale at an especially cheap price (see below 8, note 7), and it was meant to assist the eating of fruit of Shevi'it, and the trade aspect in it is secondary. And it seems that one can also learn this from the Torah law that the proceeds of fruit of Shevi'it become sanctified. Thus it gave room for a small trade for the sake of eating, since the whole prohibition of trade is learned from what is said 'for food'; if so, when a small trade helps eating it is permitted, but the Torah taught us that the money is caught up in holiness, so that the exchange for the fruit too will serve for eating.<br>And indeed some say that the prohibition is specifically to gather and sell, but to buy and sell is permitted (and they wanted to learn this from the Rash). And some say that the prohibition is specifically to buy and sell, but to gather and sell is permitted (see the Gra). And it seems that the great majority of the poskim agree that this makes no difference; the main point is that large trade in order to grow rich is forbidden by the Torah, and a small sale in the manner of an ordinary sale is forbidden rabbinically. And not in the manner of a sale, at a cheap price, in a way that makes clear that he is not like a merchant and the fruit is ownerless, it is permitted. And therefore they said in the Mishna (8:3): \"One does not sell fruit of Shevi'it by measure, nor by weight, nor by number…\" And the Rambam wrote, 6:3: \"So that he will not be like a merchant in fruit of Shevi'it… to make known that it is ownerless and that he takes the money to buy other food with it.\" (See Shabbat Ha-Aretz 6:1, in the notes.) And although in all these laws there are many views, I wrote in each and every law according to the view of most of the poskim, and according to the rule that emerges from the sugya as a whole and the Rishonim. And although the Gra permitted gathering and selling oneself, since in his view the prohibition is only to buy and sell oneself, I wrote according to the Rash and those who agree with him, and as is implied from the Rambam 6:2, and not like the Ḥazon Ish 13:17, Seder Shevi'it 6.<br>According to the Noda Bi-Yehuda, first edition, Even Ha-Ezer 77, the prohibition of trade applies to the seller and to one who buys in order to sell, because they trade in order to profit, but not to one who buys for his household. However, there is in this a prohibition of 'before the blind' and of assisting a transgression. And so wrote the Kapot Temarim (Sukka 39a) and Minḥat Shlomo 1:44. And from the words of the Makneh (Kiddushin 52a) it emerges that the prohibition of trade applies also to one who buys for his household's eating."}]},{"slug":"16-04-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-04-07/","title_he":"ז – תפיסת הדמים בקדושת שביעית","title_en":"7. The Proceeds Take On the Holiness of Shevi'it","body_en":"<p>The fruit of Shevi'it is meant for eating, as it is said (Vayikra 25:6): \"And the Sabbath produce of the land shall be food for you.\" And this is the meaning of the holiness in it: that a person may not waste it or trade in it, for it is dedicated to eating. And all that is permitted to sell is a little fruit of Shevi'it for the sake of eating. Therefore, when fruit of Shevi'it is sold, it remains in the holiness of its purpose for eating, and the money received for it also becomes sanctified and takes on the holiness of that fruit, so that it too is designated only for buying food, and one must finish eating that food by the time of removal of the fruit by whose force it was bought. In this way it is clear that it is not possible to carry on ordinary trade in fruit of Shevi'it, for the merchant wants to earn money, whereas for fruit of Shevi'it he receives money that may be used only for buying food for the near future, that is, until the time of removal.</p>\n<p>This halakha is learned from what is said (Vayikra 25:12): \"It shall be holy to you; you shall eat its produce out of the field.\" Since the fruit is holy, it takes hold of its proceeds like other holy things, that is, the money paid for the fruit becomes sanctified for the original purpose of the fruit. But unlike other things that were consecrated, such as fruit of ma'aser sheni and things consecrated to the Temple, which, when redeemed for money, became ordinary, the fruit of Shevi'it always remains in its holiness; for it is said \"it shall be holy,\" to teach us that it will always remain in its state, always dedicated to eating and not to waste and not to trade. But the money with which the fruit was bought can be redeemed: if one bought another food with it, the holiness passed from the money to the food. This is because the money became sanctified through a human act, and consequently it can be exchanged for something else. But the fruit, which became sanctified by the commandment of the Torah because it grew in Shevi'it, always remains in its holiness.</p>\n<p>And this is the law: if he sold figs of Shevi'it, the figs remained in their holiness and the money too took on their holiness, so that only foods may be bought with it. If he bought meat with the money, the holiness passed from the money to the meat and the money became ordinary. If he exchanged the meat for fish, the holiness passed from the meat to the fish and the meat became ordinary. If he exchanged fish for wine, the holiness passed to the wine. If he exchanged wine for oil, the holiness passed to the oil. It follows that the holiness of Shevi'it applies to the figs that grew in Shevi'it and to the oil, which is the last thing bought or exchanged with their proceeds, and one must finish eating the oil by the time of removal of the figs by whose force it was bought (Sukka 40b; Rambam 6:6-7).</p>\n<p>The holiness of the first fruit passes to money or to another food only by way of a purchase made between buyer and seller, or by way of an exchange between two people. But not by way of redemption; that is, it is not possible for the owner of the fruit to exchange it for another food that is in his possession. And the reason for this is simple: no benefit for the eating of the fruit will come from the redemption, since in any case the original fruit remains with him in its holiness. But when he receives fruit or money in exchange, he can thereby eat additional foods, and the fruit of Shevi'it that passed to his fellow will also be eaten in holiness. But the holiness of the money that was sanctified in the first sale can be transferred also by way of redemption: for example, he may redeem the money he received for the figs on cheese in his house, and by this the holiness will pass from the money to the cheese, which he will eat before the time of removal of the figs, and he can use the money for anything. And likewise, if he exchanged the figs of Shevi'it with his fellow for oil, the owner of the oil may say: \"Let the oil be redeemed on meat,\" and he will eat the meat by the time of removal of the figs, and he can use the oil without restriction (Sukka 40b; Rambam 6:6-8).</p>\n<p>One does not buy animal food with the proceeds of Shevi'it; and even if he sold fruit meant for animal food, once it was turned into money it became like ordinary fruit of Shevi'it meant for human food, which is the main aim of the fruit of Shevi'it. And likewise one who sells oil that may be used for lighting a lamp, and bought other oil with the money, may not use it for lighting a lamp, because the proceeds of Shevi'it are designated for human food, which is the main use of Shevi'it (Tosefta 5; 19, 5, 4; Rambam 5:12; 5:8).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"16-04-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-04-08/","title_he":"ח – עוד מאיסורי סחורה","title_en":"8. More Prohibitions of Trade","body_en":"<p>As we have learned, since the fruit of Shevi'it is meant for eating only, as it is said (Vayikra 25:6): \"And the Sabbath produce of the land shall be food for you,\" it is forbidden to trade in it, and only for the sake of eating is it permitted to sell a little of it in order to buy food with the proceeds. Therefore, included in the prohibition of trade, it is forbidden to buy with fruit of Shevi'it or its proceeds clothes, vessels, land, or anything that is not fit for eating (Mishna Shevi'it 8:8; Rambam 6:10; Ramban, Avoda Zara 62a).</p>\n<p>And likewise it is forbidden to pay debts with fruit of Shevi'it or its proceeds. And likewise it is forbidden to pay with it membership dues to the synagogue, or a debt that the members of the community are obliged to pay to the charity fund (Mishna 8:4; Rambam 6:10).</p>\n<p>And likewise it is forbidden to pay workers' wages with fruit of Shevi'it or its proceeds: if a worker repaired something in his house, he should not pay him his wages with it. But if the worker was his friend, so that he could ask him to repair the thing for free, he may even give him some of the fruit of Shevi'it in his hands as a gift. For as long as he is not paying his debt with it, there is no prohibition. And of course the worker must preserve the holiness of the fruit of Shevi'it: not to waste it, not to trade in it, and to remove it at the time of removal (Mishna 8:5; Rambam 6:11).</p>\n<p>If he transgressed and bought a shirt with fruit of Shevi'it, he should set against it a food that will become sanctified with the holiness of that fruit, and eat it by the time of removal. And likewise one who transgressed and used fruit of Shevi'it for another purpose that is not eating, for example if he anointed hides with oil of Shevi'it, should eat foods in the holiness of Shevi'it to the value of the oil (Mishna Shevi'it 8:8; Rambam 6:10).</p>\n<p>By the basic law it would have been permitted to buy with the proceeds of Shevi'it a live domestic animal, wild animal or bird, since they are fit for eating. But the Sages forbade buying them alive, lest he keep them and raise flocks from them and find it hard to preserve the holiness of Shevi'it in them (Sukka 40b; Rambam 6:9).</p>\n<p>It is permitted to send fruit of Shevi'it to a friend as a gift, on condition that he informs him that it is fruit of Shevi'it, so that he will know that he need thank him to a lesser degree, since he did not give him his own fruit but shemita fruit that was made ownerless for all; and also so that he will know to treat it according to the law of fruit of Shevi'it.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"16-04-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-04-09/","title_he":"ט – בהבלעה והקפה הכסף אינו מתקדש","title_en":"9. When Folded into Another Price, or Sold on Credit, the Money Is Not Sanctified","body_en":"<p>When a permitted sale of fruit of Shevi'it is carried out, that is, a small sale for the sake of eating and not in the manner of trade, and one does not want the money to take on the holiness of Shevi'it, its payment can be folded into something else (havla'a). For example: one who buys from his fellow apples of Shevi'it and meat, and the price of each of them is a hundred shekels, may agree with him that the payment for the meat will be two hundred shekels and the fruit will be given as a gift; and by this the money will not take on the holiness of Shevi'it.</p>\n<p>And likewise, when a person comes to buy an etrog of Shevi'it and a lulav, and the price of each of them is a hundred shekels, but he is wary of paying the seller for the etrog, lest the seller not know how to treat properly the money that will take on the holiness of Shevi'it, he should tell the seller that he is paying two hundred shekels for the lulav and ask for the etrog as a gift (Rashi, Sukka 39a). And some say that he need not say that the etrog is a gift, but it is enough that he says that the total payment is two hundred shekels, and the buyer intends that it is for the lulav (see Rambam 8:11).</p>\n<p>According to many, in addition to the fact that with havla'a the money does not take on the holiness of the fruit, there is also no prohibition of trade in this, and therefore it is permitted to sell fruit of Shevi'it by way of havla'a. However, in any case it is forbidden to trade in fruit of Shevi'it as in all the years in order to profit; rather the permission is to sell a little, and in a place that is not fixed in the market, in order to help the food supply and not in order to grow rich, as explained in the note.<sup>8</sup></p>\n<p>Also when the sale is made on credit, the money does not take on the holiness of the fruit. For since the money is paid after the fruit has already been eaten, it cannot take on its holiness. And likewise, when the sale is carried out with a post-dated cheque or with a credit card, the money that enters the account does not take on holiness.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"Tosafot, Sukka 39a, s.v. 'Ve-leitiv', wrote that there is no prohibition of trade with havla'a. And so wrote the Rash, Shevi'it 7:3, s.v. 'Yerushalmi'; Shabbat Ha-Aretz 5:13, 1, 2; 6:1, 3, 5; 8:11, 2. However, in my humble opinion the whole permission applies only to the part forbidden rabbinically: that is, they forbade even trade not for the purpose of growing rich, within a limited framework, and permitted it only with a significant change, that he gathers and his son sells, or that he sells what was left over from what he ate. But with havla'a one may gather and sell, since by the havla'a he departs from the way of trade. And similarly it is implied from Shabbat Ha-Aretz 5:13, 1, 2, that the permission of havla'a is when he does not sell in a fixed place. And likewise in the Ḥokhmat Adam, Sha'arei Tzedek 18:4, he permitted this only occasionally. And similarly the Az Nidberu 4:9 wrote that they did not permit sale by havla'a for an actual merchant in order to profit.<br>Some say that it is permitted to fold fruit of Shevi'it into packaging worth a peruta (Maran Rav Kook, Rav Tykocinski), and some are stringent that the havla'a must be in something worth more (Tzitz Ha-Kodesh, vol. 1, 15; and see Ma'amar Mordechai 17:9)."},{"n":9,"en":"In Avoda Zara 62b it is explained that with credit the proceeds do not take on holiness. And indeed according to the Ri, even if the fruit still exists, since the payment was after the taking of the fruit, it is like the payment of a debt and does not take on the holiness of the fruit; and the Ḥazon Ish relied on him, since Shevi'it is rabbinic. But according to Rashi, the Ramban, the Ran and the Meiri, only when the fruit was eaten before the time of payment does the money not take on the holiness of the fruit, for then there is nothing for it to take hold of. And so wrote Maran Rav Kook in Shabbat Ha-Aretz 8:20; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 29:24. Regarding a cheque, the Ma'amar Mordechai 17:9 wrote that if it is post-dated, the money received for it does not take on the holiness of the fruit. And in the Or Le-Tziyon (2:10) he wrote that it does not take on holiness even when it is not post-dated."}]},{"slug":"16-04-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-04-10/","title_he":"י – ביעור פירות שביעית","title_en":"10. Removal of the Fruit of Shevi'it","body_en":"<p>The fruit of Shevi'it is ownerless for all, and anyone who wishes may gather of it the amount that people are accustomed to gather or buy for their household use (above halakha 1). From the outset every person must finish his fruit by the time that fruit is gone from the fields. For if not, it implies that he took for himself more fruit of Shevi'it than he needed and harmed the ability of other people to gather fruit for themselves. And in any case, one who still has in his hands fruit of a species that is gone from the field, since people and animals can no longer gather of it and eat it, is obliged to remove the fruit from his house. And as is the law of the fruit, so is the law of the money received for it, which is called the proceeds of Shevi'it.</p>\n<p>This mitzva is learned from what is said (Shemot 23:11): \"But the seventh year you shall let it rest and lie fallow, that the poor of your people may eat; and what they leave the beast of the field shall eat.\" The Sages expounded (Mekhilta de-Rashbi there): \"As long as the beast eats from the field, you eat from the house; when it is gone from the field, finish it from the house.\" And likewise is the law regarding animal food, as it is said (Vayikra 25:7): \"And for your cattle, and for the beasts that are in your land, shall all its increase be for food.\" The Sages expounded (Pesaḥim 52b): \"As long as the wild beast eats from the field, feed the animal in the house; when it is gone for the beast in the field, finish it for your animal from the house.\"</p>\n<p>And how does one remove? He leaves for himself the quantity that the members of his household are accustomed to eat in three meals, and from the remainder he distributes food for three meals to each and every one of his acquaintances until the fruit is finished. By this he removes the fruit through eating according to its original purpose; for what people are accustomed to eat in three meals is considered removed, since it is designated for eating soon and not for storing (Mishna 9:8). And if he was too lazy to distribute the fruit in his hands, he should take it out and announce publicly that anyone who wants to take of it may come and take (Tosefta Shevi'it 8:2). And at the least he should announce this before three people, none of whom eats at his table. Then any person may take of it as much as he likes, even much more than food for three meals, on condition that he treats it with holiness as fruit of Shevi'it, that he does not waste it and does not trade in it. And even the one who made it ownerless may acquire it and return it to his house.<sup>10</sup></p>\n<p>When the owner of the fruit is pressed for money and needs the fruit, the Sages suggested to him that he take the fruit out to the street and make it ownerless before three of his friends, who will not come to acquire the fruit since they know that he wants it, and so he can go back and acquire it. And one who has no friends, and is afraid that if he makes the fruit ownerless before three people they will jump and acquire the fruit, can take the fruit out to the street at a time when there are no passers-by there, announce aloud that the fruit is ownerless, and before people have time to come and take it, lift it and bring it back into his house (Yerushalmi 9:4).</p>\n<p>A person who took many bottles of wine from an 'otzar beit din' and paid for the handling of them may from the outset make them ownerless before his friends and acquire them anew and bring them into his house; for since he volunteered to take part in the 'otzar beit din', it is fitting that he not lose what he paid for the handling of the wine.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"According to the Rambam (7:3), following his reading of the Mishna, the removal is that he burns the fruit or throws it into the sea, and it is forbidden to eat it after the time of removal. And so wrote the Meiri, and similarly wrote the Ra'avad and the Ritva. But in practice, the view of the great majority of the Rishonim and Aḥaronim is that the removal is making it ownerless, as the view of Rabbi Yosi in Mishna 9:8, that the ownerlessness is for the poor and the rich alike, and anyone who acquires it may eat it as he likes without any time limit, provided that he preserves its holiness. And so wrote the Rash, the Yere'im, the Ramban, the Rosh, Tosafot, the Ran, the Radbaz, the Mabit, Rash Sirillo, Pe'at Ha-Shulḥan and many others.<br>The Rambam wrote, 7:3: \"If he had much fruit, he distributes food for three meals to each and every one…\" And it is obvious that there is no obligation for each one to finish the food of the three meals on that day; rather, since he has only food for three meals, he is not considered as one who keeps fruit of Shevi'it for himself after it is gone from the field. And indeed the Shevitat Ha-Sadeh 14:6 holds that one must finish the food of the three meals in one day, but many disagreed with this, for the food of three meals is considered removed since it is meant for eating within a short time (and so wrote the Mikdash David 59:2; notes to Shabbat Ha-Aretz 7:3, 6; Ma'amar Mordechai 21:15; Mishpetei Eretz 30, 1; Ḥut Shani p. 355).<br>The Ramban and the Ran were in doubt whether the mitzva of removal is from the Torah; but according to the great majority of the poskim the mitzva of removal is from the Torah, and so wrote the Behag, Rashi, Tosafot, the Rash, the Rosh and others. And so it is proved from the view of the Rambam and those who agree with him; for if not, how could they have ruled that the removal is destroying fruit of Shevi'it, against the Torah, which commanded not to destroy it (Maran Rav Kook in Shabbat Ha-Aretz 7:3).<br>In Sha'arei Tzedek 19:6 he wrote that from the outset it is desirable to be meticulous according to the Rambam and remove the fruit by destroying it. And so wrote the Or Le-Tziyon 3:4. But Maran Rav Kook wrote (Mishpat Kohen 83) that this is no meticulousness at all, for if he burns it, according to most of the poskim he will transgress the Torah prohibition of destroying fruit of Shevi'it and 'bal tashḥit'.<br>However, it is clear that from the outset it is good to take into account the view of the Rambam and those who agree with him, and to plan to finish eating the fruit by the removal; and if he did not finish, he should distribute from it food for three meals to each of his friends. But if he was too lazy to do so, he may rely on most of the poskim, who hold that it is enough that he makes it ownerless. And in my humble opinion, it is right to distribute the fruit not only because of the dispute of the Rishonim, but because this is the aim of Shevi'it and of the removal, that the fruit be ownerless for all; and if he places it in the street, it may be that one person will take everything for himself, not according to the original intention of the Torah (and see below in note 12)."},{"n":11,"en":"In the Yerushalmi 9:4, Rabbi Yitzḥak bar Redifa asked Rabbi Yirmeya whether he may make the fruit ownerless and acquire it anew. Rabbi Yirmeya did not want to answer him but referred him to the great ones in Torah. And Rabbi Yoshiya answered that he may make it ownerless before three of his friends and acquire it anew. And the people of Kappadokia in Tzippori asked what people who have no three friends should do, and Rabbi Ami suggested to them that they make it ownerless in the market at a time when there are no passers-by there, as explained above. Thus far. And so the poskim wrote in practice, that a person may make it ownerless in this way and acquire the fruit anew. But in my humble opinion, since the Torah wanted everyone to be equal in the fruit of Shevi'it, from the outset one should make the fruit ownerless in a way that each of the passers-by will take food for three meals, and not so that the owner of the fruit will acquire it back. And only when the owner of the fruit is pressed did the question arise whether he may go back and acquire it or whether there is a stratagem in this. And since this is a hard question, Rabbi Yirmeya did not want to rule on it. And in practice the ruling went out that he may go back and acquire it, as explained in the Yerushalmi.<br>Regarding a person who has no three friends, the accepted explanation is that he must shout that the fruit is ownerless, in a way that three people will hear him but will not manage to arrive before he acquires the fruit again (Rivmatz, Tosafot Rid on Pesaḥim 51b; Kaftor Va-Feraḥ 48). And some say that there is no need to announce that the fruit is ownerless; rather, as long as he places it in a place where every passer-by understands that it is ownerless and he can take it, he may place it there when there are no people there, and by this he fulfils the removal (Rash on Mishna 9:8; Mahari Korkos; the Gra and Mahara Fulda on Yerushalmi Shevi'it 9:4).<br>Some say that in a case of need, such as with crates of wine, which are hard for their owner to carry from his house to the street, he should take them out to the stairwell, which is not locked, and there make them ownerless before three people (Rav Elyashiv, Mishpetei Eretz 31, 16). And in a pressing situation, according to the Gra (Shevi'it 9:4), one may leave the fruit in the house and say to three people that it is ownerless."}]},{"slug":"16-04-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-04-11/","title_he":"יא – זמן הביעור ודין מי שלא ביער","title_en":"11. The Time of Removal, and the Law of One Who Did Not Remove","body_en":"<p>The time of removal is when that fruit is gone from the fields. Today, when few know the state of the fields, we rely on the times that the kashrut committees publish for each fruit. However, in practice there is often a doubt as to when the fruit will be gone and the time of removal will arrive, and the solution to this is to make the fruit ownerless on the first day on which the removal may have fallen. And all those who take of the fruit after this time should intend not to acquire it, and likewise inform three of their friends that this fruit in their house is still ownerless and anyone who wishes may come and take of it. And only when the time of doubt is over should they intend to acquire the fruit.<sup>12</sup></p>\n<p>If the owner of the fruit transgressed and deliberately or through negligence left the fruit with him after the removal, some say that the fruit has become forbidden to be eaten by all of Israel. And some say that if he gives it as a gift to his fellow, his fellow can make it ownerless, and by this it will be permitted to everyone except the one who did not make it ownerless in time. And since the law of Shevi'it in our time is rabbinic, one may rely on the lenient. And if the owner of the fruit did not remove his fruit because of circumstances beyond his control or by mistake, some forbid and some permit; and since the law of Shevi'it in our time is rabbinic, the owner of the fruit may rely on the lenient and make it ownerless as soon as he remembers, and afterwards it will be permitted for him too to acquire it.<sup>13</sup></p>\n<p>Wine or fruit of Shevi'it that are in the possession of the beit din at the time of removal do not need removal, because they are not in the possession of a private person but are designated for distribution to the whole public (below 8:2, note 3).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"The Sages said (Mishna Shevi'it 9:2) that the Land is divided into three regions as regards removal: Yehuda, across the Jordan, and the Galil; and when the last fruit is gone from the fields in Yehuda, the time of removal for Yehuda has come, even though there is still fruit in the fields of the Galil (Rambam 7:9). And some say that each region too is divided into three, and each area has its own time (Rash, Tosafot Pesaḥim 52b, s.v. 'Ad').<br>The Sages further said that the time of removal of figs throughout the Land is at Ḥanukka of the eighth year, dates at Purim, grapes and wine at Pesaḥ, and olives at Shavu'ot; and the meaning is that on the eve of these festivals is the time of removal (Pesaḥim 53a; Rambam 7:11). And so many books wrote in practice, that the time of removal of wine is at Pesaḥ. And many wondered, for in practice grapes are gone from the fields in Shevat. Therefore in practice one should carry out the removal from the month of Shevat, and not intend to acquire the wine until Pesaḥ. And so ruled our Rabbis, Rav Yisraeli and Rav Shapira. However, the Ḥazon Ish 26:5 wrote that in a situation of doubt about the time of removal, one should make the fruit ownerless again every day. But the Minḥat Shlomo 51:18 rejected his words. And so wrote the Sefer Ha-Shemita 9:12; Shevitat Ha-Sadeh 14:33. And in any case, both because of these doubts and because of the view of the Rambam and those who agree with him, it is right from the outset to hurry to finish eating the fruit of Shevi'it by the time of removal."},{"n":13,"en":"Some forbid the fruit even if it was not removed because of circumstances beyond one's control; so wrote the Gedulat Mordechai 9; and so it emerges from the Mahari\"t 1:43, and the Shelah, Sha'ar Ha-Otiyot, Kuf. But in the Sefer Ḥaredim, chapter 7, he learned from the words of the Ramban that the owner of the fruit may remove it and go back and acquire it. And so wrote the Sha'arei Tzedek 19:5, and the Sefer Ha-Shemita 9:10, and he included in this also one who did not know when the time of removal was (which is similar to one who acted by mistake). And so ruled the Minḥat Shlomo 51:17.<br>According to those who forbid even when the removal was not done because of circumstances beyond one's control, it is forbidden even if he gives the fruit as a gift to his fellow, for the prohibition of the fruit is intrinsic. But according to those who permit in a case beyond one's control, if he did not remove through negligence and deliberately, although it is forbidden to the owner of the fruit, he may give the fruit as a gift to his fellow, upon whom the penalty does not fall; and his fellow will make it ownerless, and after it is ownerless everyone may acquire it except the owner of the fruit, who did not remove it through negligence or deliberately (Mishna Shevi'it 9:9; Yerushalmi 9:6; Rash Sirillo, Mishna Rishona, Minḥat Shlomo 51:17)."}]},{"slug":"16-04-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-04-12/","title_he":"יב – הוצאת פירות שביעית לחוץ לארץ","title_en":"12. Taking Fruit of Shevi'it Abroad","body_en":"<p>The Sages forbade taking fruit of Shevi'it out of the Land to outside the Land (Mishna Shevi'it 6:5). Some say that the reason for the prohibition is that the mitzva of removal must be fulfilled in the Land: just as the fruit of Shevi'it was made ownerless for all the inhabitants of the Land, who can come to the fields and pick of the fruit, so the removal too, which is meant to distribute the fruit to all again, must be fulfilled in the Land (Ra'av, Tiferet Yisrael).</p>\n<p>And some say that the prohibition is because of the concern that after they take it abroad they will forget that it is fruit of Shevi'it, and will not be careful about its holiness, and will come to waste it and trade in it (Rash of Sens and the Ra'avad on Torat Kohanim 25:7). And some say that because of the holiness of the fruit of Shevi'it, it should not be taken out of the Land (Rash Sirillo). And some say that they forbade taking it out lest the fruit be lacking for the people of the Land (Rav Ḥaim Berlin).</p>\n<p>If he transgressed and took the fruit abroad, he is not obliged to bring it back, but should eat it quickly. And if the time of its removal has come, he need not bring it back to the Land in order to remove it, but should remove it where it is (Pesaḥim 52b; Rambam 7:12).</p>\n<p>In recent generations a very great need has arisen to export fruit from the Land to abroad even in the seventh year, for there are branches that depend on export, and if for one year they do not export fruit, the competitors will take their place and very great damage will be caused to them. And since this concerns a pressing situation and a great loss, some poskim relied on various views according to which one may be lenient and export fruit of Shevi'it from the Land within the framework of an 'otzar beit din'. In any case, all the permissions they mentioned are much more strained than the 'heter mekhira'.<sup>14</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":14,"en":"In Mishna Shevi'it 6:5: \"One does not take out… fruit of Shevi'it from the Land to outside the Land.\" And in Pesaḥim 52b a baraita was brought: \"Our Rabbis taught: fruit that went out from the Land of Israel to outside the Land is removed wherever it is. Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar says: it should return to its place and be removed.\" And there in the Gemara it is told that Rav Safra took wine of Shevi'it abroad, and was in doubt whether he must bring it back to the Land, and decided that the halakha follows the first Tanna. And so the Rambam ruled (5:13), that it is forbidden to take fruit of Shevi'it abroad, and if he took it out he removes it abroad (7:12). And the Rash inclined to this in his commentary on the Mishna. And the prohibition is to take it out to outside the Land, including Syria; but to places conquered by those who came up from Egypt it is permitted to take it; therefore it is permitted to take fruit of Shevi'it to Eilat (Ma'amar Mordechai 13:47; see below 5:8, note 8). And some say that according to those who hold that the prohibition is because one must remove in the Land, there is no prohibition against taking out fruit in the amount of food for three meals; and one who wishes to rely on them may, since the law of Shevi'it in our time is rabbinic. And regarding the export of fruit in large quantities in a pressing situation, see below 8:5."}]},{"slug":"16-04-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/16-04-13/","title_he":"יג – חשוד על השביעית","title_en":"13. One Suspected Regarding Shevi'it","body_en":"<p>Just as it is forbidden to work in Shevi'it, so it is forbidden to strengthen the hands of Jews who work in Shevi'it, or to sell them work tools, for it is forbidden to strengthen the hands of transgressors. As it is said (Vayikra 19:14): \"Nor put a stumbling-block before the blind\"; and when a person assists a transgressor, he strengthens him in his sin and becomes a partner in making him stumble in the transgression. And not only this, but we were commanded to protest against the sinner, as it is said (ibid. 17): \"You shall not hate your brother in your heart; you shall surely rebuke your neighbour, and not bear sin because of him.\" And one who assists him stumbles in cancelling the mitzva of rebuke (Rambam 8:1; Sefer Ha-Mitzvot, negative 299, positive 205).</p>\n<p>Therefore it is forbidden to sell to a Jew who is suspected regarding Shevi'it agricultural tools that he is almost certain to use for kinds of work forbidden in Shevi'it. Today the great majority of farmers work within the framework of the 'heter mekhira', and it is permitted to sell them agricultural tools without restriction. And it is fitting for the seller to check that the buyer does indeed work within the framework of the 'heter mekhira'.</p>\n<p>It is forbidden to sell to one suspected regarding Shevi'it tools meant for work in the garden, which are almost certain to serve for forbidden work, since a 'heter mekhira' is not arranged for gardens. But if there is a reasonable possibility that they will serve a permitted use, that is, for permitted work or for use after Shevi'it, it is permitted (Rambam 8:2; see the notes to Shabbat Ha-Aretz there).</p>\n<p>One who buys fruit of Shevi'it from an am ha-aretz may buy from him up to the amount of food for three meals and no more. On the one hand, they were concerned that he might not know how to treat the proceeds of Shevi'it properly and would come to trade in them or keep them after the time of removal. On the other hand, since it is only a concern, they did not want to prevent him entirely from earning a living, and they also wanted to allow the buyer to obtain food for the coming day; therefore they permitted buying from him food for three meals (Sukka 39a, Rashi and Tosafot there).</p>\n<p>But from one who is known to be suspected regarding Shevi'it, that is, who grows fruit in Shevi'it or trades in fruit of Shevi'it, it is forbidden to buy even a little fruit, so as not to assist a transgression (see the end of note 7). And likewise it is forbidden to give fruit of Shevi'it, or money that has taken on the holiness of Shevi'it, to one who is not careful about their holiness (Rambam 8:10; Ma'amar Mordechai 13:49).</p>\n<p>Today we are accustomed to rely on the kashrut certificates that shops have. And any shop that has no kashrut certificate is among those suspected regarding Shevi'it, and it is forbidden to buy fruit of Shevi'it there. And if it has a kashrut certificate, whether according to the 'heter mekhira' or according to an 'otzar beit din', it is permitted to buy fruit from it without restriction. But if the fruit is from an 'otzar beit din', it must be treated with holiness.</p>\n<p>Similarly regarding buying flowers and pot plants: one should not buy them from a person who is not known, as long as he has no certificate that they were grown within the framework of the 'heter mekhira', or by non-Jews, or in greenhouses on a detached growing medium. But a person who is known as trustworthy, for example one who is known and recognised as observant, may testify about the fruit or flowers in his hands that they were grown in a permitted way, even if he has no certificate (Rambam 8:14; and see Ketif Shevi'it chapter 68).</p>","notes_en":[]}]});
